      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>A confined space kills quickly and quietly. Bad air gives no warning. Rescue is when a second person is most likely to be hurt. So entry runs on a permit and a gas log. When something goes wrong, or an inspector asks, the record must show the air was tested, entry was allowed and rescue was ready before anyone went in. The tool that matters is the one that records each reading as it happens, not afterwards.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>The Confined Spaces Regulations 1997 need a safe system of work. Where reasonably practicable, avoid entry at all.</li><li>Entry is controlled by a permit, air tests with a calibrated meter, and rescue arrangements, before anyone goes in.</li><li>Safety comes from the system and competent people, not software.</li><li>A paper permit book is hard to audit and easy to backfill. A timed log is not.</li><li>Quickler is £75 a month for each phone number on the account. Reports, the dashboard, sharing and downloads are free for everyone else.</li></ul>"
    },
